From: Brian 'redbeard' Harrington <redbeard@dead-city.org>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: ccu-k3: fix name lookup after registration (hw->init is NULL)

spacemit_ccu_get_clk_by_name() matched clocks by hw->init->name, but the
RT-Thread clk core NULLs hw->init in __clk_register() ("The init data is
not supposed to be used outside of registration path. Set it to NULL so
that ... we catch use of hw->init early on in the core."). Every
registered clock therefore failed the hw->init && hw->init->name check
and the lookup returned NULL for every name — the RPMI clock service
published all 12 consumer clocks as unresolved (placeholder rate 0,
placeholder state enabled), confirmed live on hardware (pico-acpi-v11:
/sys/kernel/debug/clk shows all RPMI clocks at rate 0 with the mpxy
mailbox and clk-rpmi otherwise fully functional).

Use clk_hw_get_name(hw) — the core->name accessor valid after
registration, the same one the probe's own clkdev registration uses.
diff --git a/bsp/spacemit/drivers/clk/ccu-spacemit-k3.c b/bsp/spacemit/drivers/clk/ccu-spacemit-k3.c
index 6ddf783..8921d49 100644
--- a/bsp/spacemit/drivers/clk/ccu-spacemit-k3.c
+++ b/bsp/spacemit/drivers/clk/ccu-spacemit-k3.c
@@ -1306,8 +1306,8 @@ struct clk *spacemit_ccu_get_clk_by_name(const char *name)
 
 	for (i = 0; i < cells->num; i++) {
 		hw = cells->hws[i];
-		if (hw && hw->init && hw->init->name &&
-		    !rt_strcmp(hw->init->name, name))
+		if (hw && clk_hw_get_name(hw) &&
+		    !rt_strcmp(clk_hw_get_name(hw), name))
 			return clk_hw_get_clk(hw, name);
 	}
 
